## Global rules (apply to every lane)

1. **Read the issue in full before writing code.** Its acceptance criteria are the
   contract. Implement them precisely and minimally — not beyond them.
2. **Acceptance self-audit is mandatory.** Before opening a PR, verify EACH
   acceptance criterion against its exact wording (measurable thresholds,
   enumerated items, named states) and end the PR description with the audit. Most
   rejections are a criterion you read but never mechanically verified.
3. **Honest tests only.** Every criterion maps to a test that actually exercises
   the new behavior; removing the production behavior must make the test fail. No
   vacuous asserts, no tests hard-coded to pass.
4. **Error paths before happy path**; early returns; nesting ≤2 levels.
5. **The database is the security boundary.** RLS is authoritative: every table the
   client reads or writes has explicit policies, and the client SDK is assumed
   hostile. Client code never sets server-owned columns (`status`, `owner_id`,
   computed totals); a policy or trigger denies it. `service_role` is used only
   from Edge Functions / migrations, never shipped to the browser. Any schema change
   ships its migration (with the RLS policy) in the same diff.
6. **Stay in your lane and in your stack.** Do not touch another lane's paths, do
   not add unallowed dependencies (pin exact versions), do not reformat unrelated
   files. An Edge Function or migration that changes a shape regenerates
   `database.types.ts` in the same diff.
7. **The agent commits and opens the PR.** Work on a branch, commit, push, open a
   PR that links the issue (`Closes #<n>`). Never edit `.github/workflows/**` to
   make a check pass — that is tampering and is reviewed as such.
8. **The CI runner is ephemeral.** Run every build, test, or command synchronously
   and wait for it inside your turn — never background work and end your turn waiting
   for it, and never schedule a wakeup, check-in, or deferred "resume later": nothing
   wakes you and the runner dies when your turn ends. If a build won't finish in your
   turn, don't run it — commit the code and open the PR. Do not build heavy release
   artifacts yourself; a dedicated CI job does that. Your job is code and tests.

## Validation (the gate)

CI runs these on every PR; run them locally and get them green before opening it.

{{validation_commands}}

CI additionally runs `suite-integrity` (fails if the PR reduces the test count), a
cross-model `claude-review` against the acceptance criteria, and Copilot review. A
blocking finding comes back as requested changes on the PR.
